  "schema_version": "bookstory.v1",
  "narrative_llm": "<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Short impact summary</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Kernels of Resistance is showing an early but credible impact profile: it has already been cited 6 times, downloaded 192 times, and it aligns with climate action and sustainability-oriented scholarship. The record points to uptake across food systems, agriculture, and environmental humanities, suggesting that the book is being used as a reference point beyond its immediate regional focus.</p>\n<h3 class=\"story-subheading\">Full narrative</h3>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Liza Grandia’s Kernels of Resistance is a 2024 open-access scholarly book from University of Washington Press that examines Mesoamerican food activism, seed sovereignty, and resistance to genetically modified crops. The impact record shows a book that is beginning to travel in scholarly conversation, with citations appearing in 2025 and 2026 and with the strongest concentration in work on agriculture, land use, and rural development. That pattern fits the book’s core concerns: it is being read as part of ongoing debates about food systems, agricultural policy, and political economy.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">The citation landscape is still modest in size, so the most accurate reading is that the book is at an early stage of scholarly uptake rather than broadly established influence. Even so, the citations are not confined to a single niche. Recent citing works include scholarship in The Journal of Peasant Studies, Journal of Agricultural Economics, Current Anthropology, and Environment and Planning E: Nature and Space, indicating that the book is resonating across political ecology, peasant studies, anthropology, and related fields. One later reference-entry also shows the book being used as a background source in work on genetically modified crops.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Usage data adds a different kind of signal. The book has recorded 192 downloads and 417 views, which suggests active reading rather than only formal citation. Because it is openly accessible, this usage may reflect engagement by researchers, students, and practitioners who can reach the text without paywall barriers. The book also appears in library holdings and has been recognized as a finalist for the ACLS Open Access Book Prize and Arcadia Open Access Publishing Award in Environmental Humanities, which reinforces its visibility in academic and open-access book ecosystems.</p>\n<p class=\"story-text\">Taken together, the evidence shows a work with clear policy and sustainability relevance, especially around climate action, seed sovereignty, and agricultural governance. The current record is strongest for scholarly and reader uptake; there is not yet event-based or public-discourse evidence in the dataset, so the safest interpretation is that Kernels of Resistance is already circulating in relevant research communities and accumulating practical significance through open access and citation, while its longer-term reach is still developing.</p>",
  "narrative_source": "template"
